WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.910
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:07.410
You are going to love this killer paper.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:13.039
In the classical case of 3D fabrication, we
first create a 3D geometry in our modeling

00:00:13.039 --> 00:00:14.949
software on a computer.

00:00:14.949 --> 00:00:20.430
Then, after we're done, we send this model
to a 3D printer to create a physical copy

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:21.430
of it.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:25.380
If we don't like an aspect of the printed
model, we have to go back to the computer

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.930
and adjust accordingly.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.810
If there are more fundamental issues, we may
even have to start over.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:35.890
And get this, with this piece of work, we
can have a low-fidelity wireframe version

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.320
printed immediately as we make changes within
the modeling software.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.809
This process we can refer to as real-time
or on-the-fly 3D printing.

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:51.519
In this work, both the hardware design and
the algorithm that runs the printer is described.

00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:56.089
This approach has a number of benefits, and
of course, a huge set of challenges.

00:00:56.089 --> 00:01:01.059
For instance, we can immediately see the result
of our decisions, and can test whether a new

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:05.570
piece of equipment would correctly fit into
the scene we're designing.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:11.040
Sometimes, depending on the geometry of the
final object, different jobs need to be reordered

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.350
to get a plan that is physically plausible
to print.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:19.900
In this example, the bottom branch was designed
by the artist first, and the top branch afterwards,

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.960
but their order has to be changed, otherwise
the bottom branch would block the way to the

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.030
top branch.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:30.750
The algorithm recognizes these cases and reorders
the printing jobs correctly.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:31.890
Quite remarkable.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:37.440
And, an alternative solution for rotating
the object around for better reachability

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.270
is also demonstrated.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:43.720
Because of the fact that the algorithm is
capable of this sort of decisionmaking, we

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:49.030
don't even need to wait for the printer to
finish a given step, and can remain focused

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:50.350
on the modeling process.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:55.720
Also, the handle of the teapot here collides
with the body - because of the limitations

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:02.820
of wireframe modeling, such cases have to
be detected and omitted.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:08.520
Connecting patches and adding differently
sized holes to a model are also highly nontrivial

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.530
problems that are all addressed in the paper.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:16.880
And this piece of work is also a testament
to the potential of solutions where hardware

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.760
and software is designed with each other in
mind.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.230
I can only imagine how many work hours were
put in this project until the final, working

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:26.770
solution was obtained.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.360
Incredible work indeed.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.280
We really just scratched the surface in this
episode, make sure to go to the video description

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.620
and have a look at the paper for more details.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.280
It's definitely worth it.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.230
Also, if you enjoyed this Two Minute Papers
episode, make sure to subscribe to the series

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:46.380
and if you're subscribed, click the bell icon
to never miss an episode.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.640
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

